<VI _name="lvai_typedef_bind.vi" description="Install a .ctl onto ONE control of a VI's or .ctl's front panel - addressed by the same index path lvai_typedef_tree.vi lists - and save the file in place.\0A\0AWHAT IT IS FOR. Clearing TypeDefVI on a copied .ctl makes it an ordinary control\2C and a Replace against a non-typedef installs the type and produces NO typedef link. That pair is the disconnect\2C and this VI is its second half. It is also how a flattened child is put back into its parent\2C which is why the whole chain has to be rebuilt from the inside out.\0A\0AWHY NOT Discon Typedef. Measured 2026-09-18\2C five times\3A {LV.Control} Discon Typedef from a helper opened without an application instance terminates LabVIEW\2C with no entry in NI's own crash log and the target file never written. The same helper shape with Replace survives. docs/typedef-disconnect.md has the table and the three refuted hypotheses.\0A\0AIT DESCENDS INTO BOTH CLUSTERS AND ARRAYS\2C the same way lvai_typedef_tree.vi does. Controls[] is declared on {LV.Cluster} and not on {LV.Array}\2C whose single child is reached through Array Element and comes back as ONE reference\3A both are read unconditionally and Select picks between them on Class Name\2C which keeps the descent one flat loop. The cast that does not apply fails harmlessly\2C its error deliberately unwired. An array contributes one segment like any other container and its only child index is 0.\0A\0ANothing is read back after the Replace. That method invalidates the reference it was called on\2C and the error would travel down the wire and stop Save.Instrument\2C leaving the edit unwritten. Verify by extracting the saved file and counting class=&quot;typeDef&quot; objects in the front panel heap - the VCTP type pool keeps STALE TypeDef wrappers that reach nothing\2C so counting those gives a false negative.\0A\0ASave.Instrument with an UNWIRED path saves in place.">
